('eng-inc001-understudy-q05', 'english', 'QT-RC-04', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 150,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc001-understudy-q05","marks":4,"skill":"vocabulary","question":"Write a synonym (a word or short phrase with the same meaning) for each of the following words as used in the passage: (a) 'hoarse' -- rough/croaky (this one is done for you as an example); (b) 'ceremony' (in 'without any of the ceremony she had expected'); (c) 'resented'; (d) 'admitted'; (e) 'genuinely'.","modelAnswer":"(b) ceremony: formality/fuss/special ritual. (c) resented: felt bitter or annoyed about. (d) admitted: confessed/acknowledged. (e) genuinely: truly/really/honestly.","passageTitle":"The Understudy","passageText":"Maya had known for three weeks that she was the understudy for the Narrator in the school production of \"The Lantern Keeper,\" and for three weeks she had been quietly certain she would never actually need to perform it. Being an understudy, as far as she understood it, mostly meant sitting in the wings with a script she barely needed to open, listening to somebody else collect the applause.\n\nThen, on the Tuesday before the final performance, Isla Bennett -- who had the part, and who had wanted it far more visibly than Maya ever had -- arrived at rehearsal with a voice reduced to a hoarse whisper and a note from her mother confirming laryngitis. Mr Ferris, the drama teacher, turned to Maya without any of the ceremony she had expected such a moment to carry. \"You know it,\" he said simply. \"You'll be ready by Thursday.\"\n\nMaya was not at all sure she agreed. She knew the words -- she had certainly heard them often enough -- but knowing something and standing in front of three hundred parents to perform it struck her as two entirely different skills.\n\nThe person who surprised her most was Ruby Adeyemi, who had auditioned for the Narrator herself back in September and had made no secret, at the time, of how disappointed she was to lose the part to Isla. Maya had quietly assumed, these last three weeks, that Ruby resented her too, simply for existing as a second possibility.\n\nOn Wednesday afternoon, though, Ruby found Maya alone in the empty hall, running the opening lines under her breath for perhaps the twentieth time that day. \"You're rushing the second line,\" Ruby said, not unkindly. \"The audience needs a second to picture the lighthouse before you tell them what happens to it.\"\n\nMaya blinked. \"I thought you'd want me to mess it up.\"\n\n\"I did, back in September,\" Ruby admitted, with a short, honest laugh. \"But that was months ago, and this isn't about September anymore. It's Thursday I'm thinking about now.\" She sat down cross-legged on the stage and patted the floor beside her. \"Run it again. Properly, this time. I'll tell you where you're rushing.\"\n\nThey stayed for forty minutes. Ruby corrected her pacing, suggested where to pause, and twice made Maya repeat a line simply because, in Ruby's words, \"you said it like you didn't believe it yet.\" By the time they finished, Maya's voice was hoarse in a different way than Isla's -- worn out from genuine use rather than illness.\n\nOn Thursday evening, waiting in the wings with the lantern prop cold and heavy in her hands, Maya did not feel fearless. Her hands were still shaking slightly when Mr Ferris gave her the nod. But she found, stepping into the light, that she did not need to feel fearless after all. She only needed to feel ready, and -- thanks to a rival who had chosen, for one Wednesday afternoon, to stop being a rival -- she genuinely was.\n\nAfterwards, Ruby was the first person to find her backstage. \"Second line was perfect,\" she said. \"Told you.\"","acceptedAnswers":["formality","fuss","special ritual","grand fuss","felt bitter about","felt annoyed about","held a grudge about","confessed","acknowledged","owned up","truly","really","honestly","actually"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'Angel English Content Foundation, Increment 001 (Decision 228). QT-RC-04, competency RC-03, passage "The Understudy". Confidence MEDIUM, EMC-3, matching the existing certified passage''s own five-item/worked-example synonym-list format exactly (migration 097 Q5).', 2, 'eng-inc001-understudy',
 'eng-inc001-qt-rc-04-understudy', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true, 'Guessing a synonym from a word''s sound or a loosely related idea rather than the meaning the word actually carries in its specific sentence context.',
 'NEAR_TRANSFER'),

('eng-inc001-understudy-q06', 'english', 'QT-RC-05', array['csse'], 'medium', 'short-answer', 120,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc001-understudy-q06","marks":2,"skill":"evidence","question":"Find a quotation from the passage that shows Ruby's feelings about Maya had changed since September. Explain what the quotation shows.","modelAnswer":"Ruby says 'that was months ago, and this isn't about September anymore', showing that she no longer holds on to her old disappointment and has chosen to think about the present situation (helping Maya for Thursday) instead.","passageTitle":"The Understudy","passageText":"Maya had known for three weeks that she was the understudy for the Narrator in the school production of \"The Lantern Keeper,\" and for three weeks she had been quietly certain she would never actually need to perform it. Being an understudy, as far as she understood it, mostly meant sitting in the wings with a script she barely needed to open, listening to somebody else collect the applause.\n\nThen, on the Tuesday before the final performance, Isla Bennett -- who had the part, and who had wanted it far more visibly than Maya ever had -- arrived at rehearsal with a voice reduced to a hoarse whisper and a note from her mother confirming laryngitis. Mr Ferris, the drama teacher, turned to Maya without any of the ceremony she had expected such a moment to carry. \"You know it,\" he said simply. \"You'll be ready by Thursday.\"\n\nMaya was not at all sure she agreed. She knew the words -- she had certainly heard them often enough -- but knowing something and standing in front of three hundred parents to perform it struck her as two entirely different skills.\n\nThe person who surprised her most was Ruby Adeyemi, who had auditioned for the Narrator herself back in September and had made no secret, at the time, of how disappointed she was to lose the part to Isla. Maya had quietly assumed, these last three weeks, that Ruby resented her too, simply for existing as a second possibility.\n\nOn Wednesday afternoon, though, Ruby found Maya alone in the empty hall, running the opening lines under her breath for perhaps the twentieth time that day. \"You're rushing the second line,\" Ruby said, not unkindly. \"The audience needs a second to picture the lighthouse before you tell them what happens to it.\"\n\nMaya blinked. \"I thought you'd want me to mess it up.\"\n\n\"I did, back in September,\" Ruby admitted, with a short, honest laugh. \"But that was months ago, and this isn't about September anymore. It's Thursday I'm thinking about now.\" She sat down cross-legged on the stage and patted the floor beside her. \"Run it again. Properly, this time. I'll tell you where you're rushing.\"\n\nThey stayed for forty minutes. Ruby corrected her pacing, suggested where to pause, and twice made Maya repeat a line simply because, in Ruby's words, \"you said it like you didn't believe it yet.\" By the time they finished, Maya's voice was hoarse in a different way than Isla's -- worn out from genuine use rather than illness.\n\nOn Thursday evening, waiting in the wings with the lantern prop cold and heavy in her hands, Maya did not feel fearless. Her hands were still shaking slightly when Mr Ferris gave her the nod. But she found, stepping into the light, that she did not need to feel fearless after all. She only needed to feel ready, and -- thanks to a rival who had chosen, for one Wednesday afternoon, to stop being a rival -- she genuinely was.\n\nAfterwards, Ruby was the first person to find her backstage. \"Second line was perfect,\" she said. \"Told you.\"","quotationRequired":["that was months ago, and this isn't about september anymore"],"validationTier":"TIER3_QUOTATION_PLUS_EXPLANATION"}$json$,
 'Angel English Content Foundation, Increment 001 (Decision 228). QT-RC-05, competency RC-02, passage "The Understudy". Confidence MEDIUM, EMC-3, matching the existing certified passage''s own explicit quotation-then-explanation format (migration 097 Q6). quotationRequired independently re-checked as an exact substring of this migration''s own passage text (case-insensitive match confirmed programmatically below).', 2, 'eng-inc001-understudy',
 'eng-inc001-qt-rc-05-understudy', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true, 'Selecting a quotation that shows Ruby being generally helpful, rather than one that specifically demonstrates the CHANGE in her feelings since September.',
 'MIXED_TRANSFER'),

('eng-inc001-understudy-q07', 'english', 'QT-RC-10', array['csse'], 'hard', 'short-answer', 120,
 $json${"id":"eng-inc001-understudy-q07","marks":2,"skill":"inference","question":"The passage describes Maya's hands as being 'still shaking slightly' just before she goes on stage. What does this detail suggest about how Maya is feeling, and why might the writer have included it rather than simply stating that Maya was nervous?","modelAnswer":"It suggests Maya is genuinely, physically nervous despite having prepared. The writer shows this through a specific physical detail rather than simply naming the emotion, which makes her nervousness feel more real and immediate to the reader, and sets up the contrast with the next sentence, where she realises she does not need to feel fearless, only ready.","passageTitle":"The Understudy","passageText":"Maya had known for three weeks that she was the understudy for the Narrator in the school production of \"The Lantern Keeper,\" and for three weeks she had been quietly certain she would never actually need to perform it. Being an understudy, as far as she understood it, mostly meant sitting in the wings with a script she barely needed to open, listening to somebody else collect the applause.\n\nThen, on the Tuesday before the final performance, Isla Bennett -- who had the part, and who had wanted it far more visibly than Maya ever had -- arrived at rehearsal with a voice reduced to a hoarse whisper and a note from her mother confirming laryngitis. Mr Ferris, the drama teacher, turned to Maya without any of the ceremony she had expected such a moment to carry. \"You know it,\" he said simply. \"You'll be ready by Thursday.\"\n\nMaya was not at all sure she agreed. She knew the words -- she had certainly heard them often enough -- but knowing something and standing in front of three hundred parents to perform it struck her as two entirely different skills.\n\nThe person who surprised her most was Ruby Adeyemi, who had auditioned for the Narrator herself back in September and had made no secret, at the time, of how disappointed she was to lose the part to Isla. Maya had quietly assumed, these last three weeks, that Ruby resented her too, simply for existing as a second possibility.\n\nOn Wednesday afternoon, though, Ruby found Maya alone in the empty hall, running the opening lines under her breath for perhaps the twentieth time that day. \"You're rushing the second line,\" Ruby said, not unkindly. \"The audience needs a second to picture the lighthouse before you tell them what happens to it.\"\n\nMaya blinked. \"I thought you'd want me to mess it up.\"\n\n\"I did, back in September,\" Ruby admitted, with a short, honest laugh. \"But that was months ago, and this isn't about September anymore. It's Thursday I'm thinking about now.\" She sat down cross-legged on the stage and patted the floor beside her. \"Run it again. Properly, this time. I'll tell you where you're rushing.\"\n\nThey stayed for forty minutes. Ruby corrected her pacing, suggested where to pause, and twice made Maya repeat a line simply because, in Ruby's words, \"you said it like you didn't believe it yet.\" By the time they finished, Maya's voice was hoarse in a different way than Isla's -- worn out from genuine use rather than illness.\n\nOn Thursday evening, waiting in the wings with the lantern prop cold and heavy in her hands, Maya did not feel fearless. Her hands were still shaking slightly when Mr Ferris gave her the nod. But she found, stepping into the light, that she did not need to feel fearless after all. She only needed to feel ready, and -- thanks to a rival who had chosen, for one Wednesday afternoon, to stop being a rival -- she genuinely was.\n\nAfterwards, Ruby was the first person to find her backstage. \"Second line was perfect,\" she said. \"Told you.\"","acceptedAnswers":["it shows her nervousness physically rather than just naming it, making it feel more real","it creates a vivid, specific image of her fear instead of a generic statement","it lets the reader see her nervousness rather than being told about it directly"],"validationTier":"TIER2_ACCEPTED_SET"}$json$,
 'Angel English Content Foundation, Increment 001 (Decision 228). QT-RC-10, competency RC-02, passage "The Understudy". Confidence MEDIUM, EMC-3, matching the existing certified passage''s own open ''what does this suggest'' word-choice-effect format (migration 097 Q11) -- the labelled-quotation TIER3 structure is deliberately not used here, mirroring that same migration''s own TIER2/TIER3 boundary rule for this Question Type.', 2, 'eng-inc001-understudy',
 'eng-inc001-qt-rc-10-understudy', 'angel_original', 'authentic_assessment_candidate', 1, true, 'Treating the detail only as a literal physical fact (her hands were cold), rather than recognising what showing, not simply naming, the emotion contributes to the reader''s understanding.',
 'FAR_TRANSFER')
